(i) **Glomus** is a genus of fungi that forms a mycorrhizal (symbiotic) association with the roots of higher plants.

(ii) The plant benefits because the fungal symbiont:
- **absorbs phosphorus from the soil** and passes it to the plant;
- gives the plant **tolerance to salinity and drought**;
- gives **resistance to root-borne pathogens**;
- brings about an overall **increase in plant growth and development**.

Cancers of the internal organs are detected by:
- **biopsy and histopathological studies** of the tissue;
- **radiography** (use of X-rays);
- **computed tomography (CT)**;
- **magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)**.

Any one of them, for example:
- **Biopsy**: a piece of the suspected tissue is cut into thin sections, stained and examined under the microscope by a pathologist (histopathological study).
- **Computed tomography** uses X-rays to generate a three-dimensional image of the internals of an object.
- **MRI** uses strong magnetic fields and non-ionising radiation to accurately detect pathological and physiological changes in the living tissue.

Eli Lilly, an American company, in 1983 prepared **two DNA sequences corresponding to the A and B chains of human insulin**, introduced them into plasmids of E. coli to produce the insulin chains, and joined the A and B chains by disulphide bonds to form mature **human insulin**. This was accepted overwhelmingly because:
- insulin taken earlier from the pancreas of slaughtered cattle and pigs caused **allergy or other reactions to the foreign protein** in some patients; the recombinant insulin is human insulin, so it is **safe, with the least side effects**;
- it can be produced **in bulk** to meet the demand of the growing number of diabetic patients;
- it **reduces the slaughter of animals** and addresses the ethical issues of using an animal product;
- for the first time insulin was **assembled in its mature form** (without the extra C peptide of pro-insulin) through r-DNA technology.
